D3 = [
# ---------------- AI PRODUCT DESIGN ----------------
("AIPD","Hard","Design a conversation flow for an AI therapist bot. What guardrails do you need?",
 "Safety-critical conversational design.","Define scope, user safety, escalation, privacy and honesty about limits.",
 "State clearly that it is a wellbeing support tool, not a clinician. Flow: welcome with scope and privacy, a short check-in, supportive listening and evidence-based exercises (breathing, journaling, cognitive reframing), and goal tracking. Guardrails: detect crisis language and immediately show local emergency and helpline options and encourage contacting a person; never diagnose or give medication advice; avoid dependency by encouraging real-world support; age gating; strict privacy (minimal data, encryption, deletion on request); human review of risky cases; and clinician-reviewed content. Test with safety evals and expert panels. Metrics: user wellbeing scores, crisis handoff accuracy, harmful-response rate (target zero), and retention without over-reliance.",
 "Letting the bot act like a licensed therapist; weak crisis handling.","Scope and disclaimers, supportive exercises, crisis detection with handoff, privacy, clinician review.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","How would you design UI for an AI image editor that combines generation and editing?",
 "Interaction design for creative tools.","Support both prompts and direct manipulation; keep control and history.",
 "Combine a canvas with a prompt bar. Users can generate, then select regions to edit by painting or clicking, with prompts like 'replace the sky'. Provide layers, undo history, versions side by side, and sliders for strength. Offer presets and reference images, and show non-destructive edits. Make costs and time clear. For beginners, suggested edits; for pros, fine controls. Measure first-image success time, edits per project, export rate and retention.",
 "Chat-only editing with no direct manipulation or history.","Canvas plus prompt, region edits, layers, history, versions, presets.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","Design an AI-powered email composer for busy executives. What's the UX?",
 "Workflow design and trust.","Speed, control, tone, and safety around sending.",
 "Executives want fast, in-voice drafts. Offer one-tap draft from a short intent ('decline politely, offer next week'), learn tone from past sent emails, show a clear diff for edits, and let them adjust length and tone quickly. Surface key facts from the thread and calendar, flag commitments and sensitive content, and never auto-send. Include shortcuts and mobile-first layouts, delegation to assistants, and an undo send. Metrics: time to send, edit rate, adoption and satisfaction.",
 "Auto-sending without review.","Intent-to-draft, voice learning, diffs, flags, never auto-send.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","Create an interaction model for an AI code review tool that works in VS Code.",
 "Developer workflow integration.","Fit existing review habits, minimise noise, give evidence.",
 "Provide inline comments with severity, explanation and a suggested fix as a diff that can be applied or dismissed. Group by type (bug, security, style, performance), allow configuration of rules, and learn from dismissed suggestions to reduce noise. Show reasoning and references to code and docs. Support a summary of the change, test suggestions and a pre-commit mode. Keep it fast and optional. Metrics: accepted suggestion rate, bugs caught, review time saved, and false-positive rate.",
 "Flooding developers with low-value comments.","Inline severity-labelled comments, apply-as-diff, learn from dismissals, config.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","Design how users would control 'creativity vs accuracy' in an AI writing assistant.",
 "Control design for probabilistic systems.","Translate technical settings to user goals; show effects.",
 "Avoid exposing 'temperature'. Offer a simple mode choice (Precise, Balanced, Creative) with plain descriptions, and context-sensitive defaults (precise for summaries, creative for brainstorming). Provide a slider only in advanced settings, with examples of how outputs change. For factual content, add source-backed mode with citations. Allow regenerate and compare variants. Test comprehension and satisfaction.",
 "Exposing raw model parameters.","Named modes, smart defaults, examples, source-backed option.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","How would you design loading states for an AI feature that takes 45 seconds?",
 "Latency UX.","Set expectations, show progress, allow multitasking.",
 "Tell users upfront ('about 45 seconds'), show meaningful progress steps (reading, analysing, drafting), stream partial results where possible, allow users to continue other work or leave and get notified, and provide a cancel option. Use skeletons and animations sparingly. If failure is possible, explain and offer retry with saved input. Measure abandonment and satisfaction by wait time.",
 "Indefinite spinner with no information.","Upfront estimate, step progress, streaming, background with notification, cancel and retry.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","Design a feedback mechanism for users to improve AI outputs over time.",
 "Feedback loops.","Make it low-effort, informative and visible in effect.",
 "Provide thumbs with optional reasons (wrong, unhelpful, unsafe, style), inline edit tracking (what users change tells more than a vote), and a 'remember this' option for preferences. Show that feedback matters ('we will use shorter replies'). Feed labelled failures into an evaluation set and prompt or model improvements. Protect privacy and let users opt out. Metrics: feedback rate, quality trend, repeat issues.",
 "Collecting votes nobody uses.","Thumbs plus reasons, edit signals, preferences, visible effect, eval pipeline.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","Create an onboarding flow for users who've never used AI before.",
 "Inclusive onboarding.","Reduce fear, show value fast, teach by doing.",
 "Start with a friendly explanation of what it can do and what it cannot, then offer three real tasks to try with one tap (write a message, plan a trip, explain a document). Provide example prompts, plain language, and guardrails explained simply (it can make mistakes, check important facts). Offer a short guided first success and a help hub. Use progressive disclosure for advanced features. Measure completion, first successful task and week-1 return.",
 "Long tutorials and jargon.","Plain intro, tap-to-try tasks, example prompts, expectations about mistakes.") ,
("AIPD","Hard","Design how an AI scheduling assistant would handle conflicts and preferences.",
 "Complex decision UX.","Collect preferences, explain trade-offs, ask for confirmation.",
 "Capture preferences (work hours, focus blocks, travel buffers, priority people) and rank importance. When a conflict arises, propose options with trade-offs ('move A to Thursday; B is lower priority'), explain reasoning, and ask for confirmation before changing others' invites. Learn from choices over time. Provide an undo and an audit trail, and respect others' privacy. Metrics: conflicts resolved without manual effort, undo rate and satisfaction.",
 "Silently rescheduling meetings.","Preference model, ranked options with reasons, confirm before change, undo.") ,
("AIPD","Hard","How would you design a 'confidence indicator' for AI-generated medical advice?",
 "Uncertainty communication in high-stakes domains.","Avoid false precision; guide action.",
 "Avoid a percentage unless calibrated. Use clear levels linked to actions: 'well supported by guidelines', 'limited evidence', 'seek a clinician'. Show sources, what information was missing, and red-flag symptoms prompting urgent care. Always include a path to a professional. Test with users and clinicians for comprehension and for reducing risky behaviour. Track whether users take appropriate action and whether the indicator is calibrated against expert review.",
 "A single number that looks scientific but is not calibrated.","Levels tied to actions, sources, missing-info prompts, clinician path, calibration.") ,
("AIPD","Hard","Design a collaboration model for multiple users working with one AI assistant.",
 "Multi-user AI design.","Shared context, permissions, attribution, conflicts.",
 "Use shared workspaces where the assistant sees agreed context and each message is attributed to a person. Provide roles and permissions, private versus shared threads, and visibility of what the AI used. Handle conflicting instructions with explicit ownership (who can approve changes) and a change log. Allow asking the assistant to summarise decisions and tasks. Metrics: active teams, shared artifacts produced, and conflicts resolved.",
 "One chat thread for everyone with no attribution.","Shared workspace, attribution, permissions, approvals, change log.") ,
("AIPD","Medium","Create UI for an AI data analyst that generates SQL and visualizations.",
 "Trust and transparency for data tools.","Show the query, the data, assumptions and allow edits.",
 "Layout: question box, generated answer with chart, and a panel showing the SQL, tables used, filters and assumptions. Allow editing the SQL or the question, switching charts, and exporting. Validate queries (row counts, sample data) and warn about ambiguous definitions with a metrics glossary. Provide permissions and data governance. Metrics: answers accepted without edits, accuracy on a test set, time to insight.",
 "Black-box numbers with no way to inspect.","Answer plus SQL and assumptions, editable, validation, governed definitions.") ,

# ---------------- AI PRODUCT STRATEGY ----------------
("AIPS","Hard","You're CEO of Anthropic. What should your product strategy be for the next 3 years?",
 "Company-level strategic thinking.","Vision, differentiation, segments, moats, sequencing and risks.",
 "A plausible strategy: be the most trusted and capable AI for work, focusing on high-value professional and enterprise use (coding, analysis, writing, agents) where reliability and safety are paid for. Year 1: strengthen core developer and enterprise offerings and make agentic workflows dependable. Year 2: deepen integrations and data connections, expand consumer and team products where retention is strong. Year 3: platform ecosystem with partners and controls for regulated industries. Moats: model quality, trust and safety, enterprise relationships and workflow integration. Risks: compute costs, competition, regulation. I would define metrics: revenue from enterprise and API, retention, and trust measures.",
 "A vague 'build the best AI' with no focus or sequencing.","Trusted work AI, coding and agents first, enterprise depth, ecosystem later; moats and metrics.") ,
("AIPS","Hard","Google has Gemini. Should they integrate it into every product or keep it separate?",
 "Platform integration strategy.","Distribution advantage vs fragmentation, quality and trust.",
 "Both: a standalone product for exploration and deep tasks, plus embedded features where they clearly improve workflows (search, email, docs, maps). Integration uses distribution but risks cluttered, inconsistent experiences and trust problems if quality varies. Set shared standards: a common model platform, design patterns and safety evaluations, with product teams choosing where AI adds real value. Measure usage and satisfaction by surface and prune weak integrations.",
 "Putting AI everywhere regardless of value.","Hybrid: standalone plus embedded where valuable; shared platform and standards.") ,
("AIPS","Hard","OpenAI has ChatGPT, DALL-E, Sora as separate products. Should they combine them?",
 "Product portfolio strategy.","User jobs, discoverability, cost and focus.",
 "Users' jobs cross modalities (write, illustrate, animate), so a unified entry point with modality as capability is attractive: one conversation, shared context and history, one subscription. Keep specialised surfaces for professionals who need controls (video editing tools). Technical and cost differences matter, so combine experiences while keeping separate backend services. Measure cross-modal use, retention and revenue per user. Risk: overloading the main product.",
 "Merging everything into one interface without considering pro workflows.","Unify entry and context, keep pro surfaces, measure cross-modal usage.") ,
("AIPS","Medium","You're PM of GitHub Copilot. Microsoft just launched 365 Copilot. How do you think about product portfolio?",
 "Portfolio coherence within a company.","Define distinct jobs, shared platform, avoid cannibalisation.",
 "Copilot for developers and Copilot for office work serve different users and workflows. I would define clear boundaries (code and engineering workflows versus documents and email), share the underlying platform for models, identity, security and billing, and create bridges where jobs overlap (documentation, project updates, requirements to code). Align branding and pricing so customers understand bundles. Metrics: adoption per persona, cross-product usage and enterprise expansion.",
 "Overlapping features and confusing pricing.","Distinct jobs, shared platform, bridges at overlaps, coherent packaging.") ,
("AIPS","Hard","Anthropic's Claude is 'safer', but OpenAI's ChatGPT has more users. How do you win as Anthropic?",
 "Competitive positioning.","Choose where safety and reliability are decisive.",
 "Do not chase mass consumer reach on its terms. Win segments where trust is a purchase driver: enterprises, regulated industries, coding and agents where mistakes are costly. Demonstrate reliability with evidence, offer controls and compliance, and make products that excel at long, complex work. Grow consumer usage through strong core experiences and partnerships, but let enterprise and developers lead. Metrics: enterprise revenue, retention, share of coding workloads and customer trust measures.",
 "Marketing safety without product proof.","Win trust-sensitive segments, prove reliability, partner for reach.") ,
("AIPS","Medium","Should an AI company build one general model or multiple specialized models?",
 "Technical-strategic trade-off.","Quality, cost, latency, maintenance and customer needs.",
 "General models offer breadth and easier maintenance; specialised models can be cheaper, faster and better in narrow domains. A common strategy is a family: a frontier general model plus smaller, faster or domain-tuned variants, with routing. Decide by customer needs (cost-sensitive high volume tasks, regulated domains), evaluation results and operational complexity. Measure quality per cost and latency by task.",
 "Choosing by ideology instead of task evidence.","Model family with routing; specialise where evals and economics justify it.") ,
("AIPS","Hard","You're PM at Meta. How should Llama (open source) fit into Meta's product strategy?",
 "Open-source strategy.","What does Meta gain from openness?",
 "Meta's core business is attention and engagement across its apps, so cheap, widely available models reduce dependence on competitors and attract developer ecosystems, talent and standards. Llama supports Meta's own products (assistants, ads tools, creator features) while the open ecosystem improves tooling. Decide what to keep open and what to differentiate: integration with Meta's data and surfaces. Risks: misuse, enabling competitors. Metrics: ecosystem adoption, product usage of Meta AI features and cost savings.",
 "Treating open source as charity.","Reduce dependence, build ecosystem, differentiate through product integration.") ,
("AIPS","Hard","Your AI startup has a great model but no distribution. OpenAI has worse tech but ChatGPT distribution. What's your strategy?",
 "Startup strategy against incumbent distribution.","Find a wedge where quality matters more than convenience.",
 "Do not fight head-on for general users. Find a segment where model quality changes outcomes (specific domain, language, latency, privacy, or cost) and sell to them directly. Use distribution partnerships: be embedded in tools others already use through APIs and plugins. Build a product around the model (workflow, data, integrations) so value is not only the model. Show proof via benchmarks on customer tasks. Metrics: wins in the chosen segment, retention and expansion.",
 "Assuming a better model sells itself.","Niche where quality decides, partner for distribution, build workflow moat.") ,
("AIPS","Medium","Should AI products be priced per-seat (like SaaS) or per-usage (like APIs)?",
 "Pricing strategy.","Value metric, cost structure, buyer preferences.",
 "Per-seat is predictable and easy to buy but misaligns with cost when AI does work without a user or usage varies widely. Per-usage aligns with cost and value but is unpredictable for buyers. A hybrid is common: seat plus included usage and overage, or outcome-based pricing where results are measurable. Choose based on how value scales, cost per task and customer preference; test with pilots.",
 "Copying SaaS pricing despite variable costs.","Hybrid seat plus usage; outcome pricing where measurable; test with pilots.") ,
("AIPS","Hard","You're PM at Notion. How should you integrate AI throughout the product versus building standalone AI features?",
 "Integration strategy in a workflow product.","Embed where the work happens; keep standalone where new jobs exist.",
 "Notion's strength is knowledge and workflow in one workspace. AI should be woven into docs, databases and search (drafting, summarising, filling properties, answering from workspace data), so value appears in context. A standalone assistant makes sense for cross-workspace questions and automation. Prioritise by frequency and value, respect permissions and privacy, and make AI results editable. Pricing as an add-on or bundled depends on adoption. Metrics: AI feature usage per active user, retention and expansion.",
 "Bolting on a chatbot unrelated to the workflow.","Embed in docs and databases, standalone assistant for cross-workspace tasks, respect permissions.") ,
("AIPS","Hard","Microsoft owns OpenAI's tech. Google built Gemini in-house. Which strategy is better for the next 5 years?",
 "Strategic comparison.","Speed, control, dependency, ecosystem and risk.",
 "Partnering gave Microsoft speed to market and frontier capability with less upfront research risk, at the cost of dependence and shared economics. Building in-house gives Google control, integration with hardware and data, and cost advantages at scale, but demands heavy investment and execution. Over five years the winner depends on model quality parity, distribution, cost per query and flexibility; a hedged approach (own models plus partnerships) reduces risk. I would judge by product performance, margins and ecosystem adoption rather than origin.",
 "Declaring a winner without criteria.","Compare on speed, control, dependency, cost; hedge strategy.") ,
("AIPS","Medium","Your AI company needs to decide: improve the model or ship more features? How do you decide?",
 "Prioritisation between platform and product.","Where is the binding constraint on user value?",
 "Diagnose why users are not getting value: model quality (errors, capability limits) or product gaps (workflow, UX, integrations). Use evaluation data and user research. If quality blocks key use cases, model improvements have the highest leverage; if users are happy with quality but cannot complete jobs, features win. Balance with a portfolio: allocate a share to model and platform and a share to product, and review quarterly. Metrics: task success, retention drivers and cost.",
 "Treating it as either-or without evidence.","Find the binding constraint with evals and research; portfolio allocation.") ,

# ---------------- AI SAFETY & ETHICS ----------------
("AISE","Hard","Users are using ChatGPT to write phishing emails. How do you stop this without breaking legitimate use?",
 "Dual-use safety trade-offs.","Detect intent and patterns; add friction by risk; protect benign users.",
 "Use layered defences: intent and content classifiers for clear phishing attempts, policies that refuse impersonation of institutions or credential harvesting, rate limits and account signals for abuse, and monitoring for patterns across accounts. Provide safe alternatives (security awareness training templates). Keep false positives low for marketing and security professionals with verified accounts and clear usage policies. Measure abuse detection, false-positive rate on benign emails and user complaints. Iterate with red teaming.",
 "Blocking all email writing or ignoring dual use.","Layered classifiers, policies, rate limits, verified pro paths, red teaming.") ,
("AISE","Hard","Your AI image generator is being used to create deepfakes. You have 2 weeks to ship a solution. What do you do?",
 "Rapid safety response.","Triage, quick mitigations, then longer-term controls.",
 "Within two weeks: block generation of real people's likeness for sensitive categories (public figures in deceptive contexts, sexual content, minors), add detection and prompt filters, enable reporting and rapid takedown, add visible and invisible watermarking or provenance metadata, and rate-limit suspicious accounts. Communicate policy clearly. Longer term: consent-based likeness features, partnerships on detection standards, audits. Metrics: abuse volume, time to takedown and false positives.",
 "Only relying on terms of service.","Likeness blocks, filters, provenance, reporting and takedown, rate limits, comms.") ,
("AISE","Hard","An AI product you built is showing bias against certain demographics. How do you fix it at the product layer?",
 "Mitigating bias without retraining.","Measure, mitigate and monitor at the product layer.",
 "Measure with slice-based evaluation across demographics and contexts. Product-layer fixes: adjust prompts and instructions, add post-processing rules and output checks, improve retrieval data, apply thresholds by use case, offer human review for high-stakes decisions, and show uncertainty. Provide user feedback channels and publish limitations. Monitor continuously and escalate model-level issues to research. Metrics: disparity measures and complaint rates.",
 "Assuming a single fix removes bias.","Slice evals, prompt and rule mitigations, human review, monitoring.") ,
("AISE","Hard","Design a content moderation system for an AI chatbot that millions of kids will use.",
 "Child safety design.","Strict policies, layered filters, parental controls, escalation.",
 "Design for the strictest standards: age-appropriate model and content settings, input and output classifiers for sexual, violent, self-harm and grooming content, topic restrictions, no personal data collection beyond need, and parental controls and reports. Add crisis detection with safe responses and escalation, human review of flagged conversations, audit logs, and regular red teaming with child safety experts. Comply with children's privacy laws. Metrics: violation rate, false negatives found in audits, escalation response time and parent satisfaction.",
 "Relying on a single filter.","Layered filters, restricted topics, crisis handling, parental controls, audits and compliance.") ,
("AISE","Hard","Your AI hiring tool is 95% accurate but has subtle gender bias. How do you address this?",
 "Fairness in high-stakes decisions.","Quantify, mitigate, change the decision role of AI, and govern.",
 "Accuracy is not enough. Measure outcomes by gender (selection rates, error rates), investigate causes (data, features such as proxies, labels), and mitigate: remove proxies, rebalance, set fairness constraints, and calibrate. Change the product role to decision support with human review, explanations and audit logs. Comply with employment laws and notify candidates. Monitor continuously and run external audits. If bias persists, pause use for that decision step.",
 "Shipping because overall accuracy is high.","Measure by group, mitigate causes, human oversight, audits, legal compliance.") ,
("AISE","Hard","Users are jailbreaking your AI model to generate harmful content. What product solutions do you implement?",
 "Adversarial safety.","Defence in depth; monitor and adapt.",
 "Layers: stronger system instructions, input and output classifiers, refusals with safe alternatives, rate limits and account-level enforcement, detection of known jailbreak patterns, red teaming and bug bounties, and rapid patching with evaluation sets built from discovered attacks. Monitor for novel attacks and share learnings with the safety team. Balance safety with usability by tracking false refusals. Metrics: jailbreak success rate on a test suite, time to patch and benign refusal rate.",
 "A single filter; ignoring false refusals.","Layered defences, red teaming, evals from attacks, rapid patching, monitor false refusals.") ,
("AISE","Hard","Your AI health advisor sometimes gives dangerous medical advice. How do you ship while staying safe?",
 "Shipping in high-risk domains.","Narrow scope, guardrails, human oversight, evidence.",
 "Narrow the scope to low-risk guidance (education, lifestyle, preparing for visits), route symptoms with red flags to urgent care and clinicians, ground answers in vetted sources with citations, add disclaimers and clear limits, and use clinician-reviewed evaluation sets with zero tolerance for dangerous failures. Include human escalation and incident response. Release gradually with monitoring. If safety cannot meet thresholds, do not ship that capability. Metrics: dangerous response rate on evals, escalation accuracy and user outcomes.",
 "Shipping with a disclaimer only.","Narrow scope, vetted sources, red-flag escalation, clinician evals, staged release.") ,
("AISE","Medium","Design a system to make AI-generated content transparently labeled while keeping good UX.",
 "Transparency design.","Label clearly, consistently and unobtrusively; combine visible and technical signals.",
 "Use visible, consistent labels (a small badge or caption) on AI-generated or AI-edited content, with a details view explaining what was generated. Add invisible provenance (watermarks and content credentials) that persist when shared. Let creators label and disclose easily, and automate labelling for content created inside the product. Design for context: strong labels for realistic media and news-like content, lighter for obvious creative work. Test comprehension and ensure labels do not harm usability. Metrics: label coverage and user understanding.",
 "Burying disclosure or relying only on removable labels.","Visible labels plus provenance, easy creator disclosure, context-based prominence.") ,

# ---------------- AI METRICS & PRICING ----------------
("AIMP","Hard","Design a full metrics framework for an AI code assistant. What's the north star?",
 "Metrics design for an AI product.","North star, input metrics, quality, trust, economics and guardrails.",
 "North star: weekly number of developers who ship code with accepted AI help, or accepted suggestions that survive to merge (a quality-adjusted measure). Inputs: activation (first accepted suggestion), suggestions shown and accepted, acceptance by language and task, latency. Quality: code that survives review, test pass rate, bugs introduced, edit distance. Trust: opt-out rate, satisfaction, frequency of reverts. Business: retention, seats and expansion, cost per active developer. Guardrails: security issues, license and copyright flags, developer productivity measured independently.",
 "Using acceptance rate alone.","North star: merged AI-assisted code; inputs, quality, trust, economics, guardrails.") ,
("AIMP","Hard","How would you price ChatGPT if you were launching it today? What's your pricing strategy?",
 "Pricing from first principles.","Costs, value, segments, competition and willingness to pay.",
 "Start with cost per use (inference is a real marginal cost) and value by segment. Offer a free tier to build the market with limits, a paid individual plan priced near comparable subscriptions that gives higher limits and better models, team and enterprise plans with admin and security, and API usage pricing for developers. Test price points and bundles, use usage caps to protect margin, and consider regional pricing. Track conversion, churn, gross margin and the free-to-paid ratio.",
 "Setting price without understanding marginal cost.","Free tier, individual plan, team and enterprise, API; test and protect margin.") ,
("AIMP","Hard","Your AI API costs $0.10 per request but competitors charge $0.01. How do you compete?",
 "Competing at a price gap.","Understand quality difference, reduce cost, differentiate, segment.",
 "First check if the offerings are comparable on quality, latency and reliability. If yes, reduce cost: model optimisation, caching, smaller models for easy requests, batching and better infrastructure. If you are better, segment customers for whom quality matters, justify price with outcomes (accuracy, support, SLAs), and offer volume tiers. Consider a lite product at a lower price. Show total cost of ownership including errors and rework. Metrics: win rate, margin and churn.",
 "Matching price and losing money.","Compare quality, cut cost, segment on value, tiered product line.") ,
("AIMP","Hard","Create a freemium model for Claude where 98% of users stay free but you're still profitable.",
 "Unit economics of freemium.","Control cost per free user, monetise the high-value 2%.",
 "Model it: if free users cost X per month and 2% pay Y, profitability requires Y x 2% > X, so keep free usage cheap (smaller models, limits, caching, lower priority) and make paid value high (higher limits, best models, long context, projects, team features). Use enterprise and API revenue to subsidise. Reduce abuse. Track cost per free user, conversion, revenue per paying user, and margin by cohort. Free serves marketing and data on use cases rather than being a charity.",
 "Ignoring the cost of free users.","Cheap free tier, valuable paid tier, enterprise and API subsidy, monitor unit economics.") ,
("AIMP","Hard","What metrics would tell you if an AI product has product-market fit vs traditional products?",
 "PMF measurement for AI.","Traditional signals plus AI-specific quality and trust.",
 "Traditional signals still apply: retention curves that flatten, organic growth, the 'very disappointed' survey, and willingness to pay. AI-specific: task success and acceptance rates, repeat use for the same job, edit distance, reliance behaviours, and cost per successful task, because high usage can hide poor quality or unsustainable cost. Also watch novelty decay (usage spikes then drops). Segment by use case to find where PMF exists.",
 "Using usage growth alone.","Retention and survey plus task success, repeat jobs, cost per success, novelty decay.") ,
("AIMP","Hard","Design an A/B test to measure if a new AI feature actually improves user outcomes (not just engagement).",
 "Experiment design.","Outcome metrics, guardrails, randomisation and duration.",
 "Define the outcome (time to complete tasks, task success, resolution, conversion, quality ratings) rather than clicks. Randomise users, keep the treatment minimal, pre-register primary and guardrail metrics (errors, complaints, latency, cost), and run long enough to cover weekly cycles and novelty effects. Measure outcomes with independent signals such as human-rated quality or downstream results (fewer support tickets, successful purchases). Segment by user type, check sample ratio, and compute confidence intervals. Decide using impact and cost.",
 "Measuring engagement only.","Outcome metrics, randomisation, guardrails, novelty-aware duration, independent quality signal.") ,
]
